Excellent catch — that error:

> ❌ **“book id is never assigned to type ‘never’”**

is a **TypeScript type inference issue**, not a React logic problem.

Let’s walk through **why it happens** and how to fix it properly 👇

---

## 🧠 Why It Happens

If your project uses **TypeScript** (`.tsx` file), this line:

```tsx
const [books, setBooks] = useState([]);
```

initializes an **empty array**, and TypeScript doesn’t know what type it should contain.
So it infers it as `never[]`, meaning:

> “An array that will never have any valid element type.”

Then later when you do:

```tsx
const newBook = { id: Date.now(), title, author };
setBooks([...books, newBook]);
```

TypeScript complains:

> ❌ You’re trying to push `{ id, title, author }` into an array of type `never[]`.

---

## ✅ Fix #1 — Define a Book Type (Recommended)

Add an interface and tell React what type the array holds:

```tsx
interface Book {
  id: number;
  title: string;
  author: string;
}

const [books, setBooks] = useState<Book[]>([]);
```

✅ This way, `books` is an array of objects with `id`, `title`, and `author`.

---

## ✅ Fix #2 — Inline Type Annotation (Quick Fix)

If you don’t want to define an interface, you can write:

```tsx
const [books, setBooks] = useState<{ id: number; title: string; author: string }[]>([]);
```

Same effect, just inline.

---

## ✅ Fix #3 — Use `useState<any[]>([])` (Not Recommended)

You can silence the error with:

```tsx
const [books, setBooks] = useState<any[]>([]);
```

…but this **disables type safety**, so it’s only okay for fast debugging.

